Q: What are Beans in Spring Boot?

A **bean** is an object created and managed by the Spring IoC container
(service, repository, controller, config object, etc.).

You typically don’t `new` these yourself — Spring creates, wires, and destroys them.

────────────────────────────────────────
1) How beans are defined

Stereotypes (component scan):
- `@Component` — generic bean
- `@Service` — business layer
- `@Repository` — persistence layer (also exception translation)
- `@Controller` / `@RestController` — web layer

Explicit factory method:
@Configuration
class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    MyComponent myComponent() { return new MyComponent(); }
}

(XML `<bean>` still exists in legacy apps; uncommon in Boot.)

────────────────────────────────────────
2) Bean scopes

| Scope       | Meaning                                      |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| singleton   | One instance per Spring container (**default**) |
| prototype   | New instance every getBean/injection         |
| request     | One per HTTP request (web)                   |
| session     | One per HTTP session (web)                   |
| application | One per ServletContext                       |
| websocket   | One per WebSocket session                    |

@Component          // singleton by default
public class CartService { }

@SessionScope
@Component
public class UserSessionData { }

────────────────────────────────────────
3) Lifecycle (simplified)

1. Container starts / refreshes
2. Bean definition loaded
3. Instantiation
4. Dependency injection
5. Initialization (`@PostConstruct` / `InitializingBean`)
6. Bean in use
7. Destruction on shutdown (`@PreDestroy` / `DisposableBean`) — mainly for singletons

@Component
public class LifecycleBean {
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() { System.out.println("initialized"); }

    @PreDestroy
    public void destroy() { System.out.println("destroyed"); }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
4) Autowiring / DI

Preferred: **constructor injection** (immutable, testable)
Also: setter (optional deps), field `@Autowired` (works, less preferred)

@Service
public class MyService {
    private final MyComponent myComponent;

    public MyService(MyComponent myComponent) { // no @Autowired needed if one constructor
        this.myComponent = myComponent;
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“A Spring bean is a container-managed object. We declare it with @Component/@Service/@Repository/@Controller or @Bean, default scope is singleton, and Spring handles creation, dependency injection, and lifecycle callbacks.”
